#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068657 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "623f9081-96b8-53f5-8881-b17bea5d0316" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method"}] :question/id #uuid "29e835eb-531e-5241-911f-45e2d686b183" :question/math-academy-id "e-8853" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=2y-2y^2$ and the $y$-axis, as shown below. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/c3/c34f58201d91a5490379127a64782b05bd7e7bdea7723109cee11587d019cb29.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-2{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2y(1-y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0,$ and $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =4\\pi ((\\frac{1}{3}-0)-(\\frac{1}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068658 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49361606-6120-5b9c-888f-0cdaf012f125" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid"}] :question/id #uuid "77659797-1ecc-567e-b864-cfb39c21b0c0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8856" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cf4e624293586613b00a7209cd40a89b5303f0495c2fba36d751660ae7f8a91b.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=4y-y^2$ and the $y$-axis, as shown above. Using the shell method, find an integral that gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(4-y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0, \\, 4.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=4.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}y(4y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49361606-6120-5b9c-888f-0cdaf012f125" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c6a9ef1-58fa-5280-a803-6c73dd0c4040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}{(-4+5y-{y}^{2})}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531106} {:answer/id #uuid "97b0c3d5-9428-5059-9aac-4e5b185105dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{(-4+5y-{y}^{2})}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531189} {:answer/id #uuid "9ed39119-aac9-5284-a6df-cb94eea8b8c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}(-4+5y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531206} {:answer/id #uuid "db0b3956-a9bc-5908-adb2-c6941993f772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}y(-4+5y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531315} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a197b4-bd42-5a30-a39c-ab3ec0c5de82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}y(-4+5y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1a197b4-bd42-5a30-a39c-ab3ec0c5de82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}y(-4+5y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531357} :answer-field/id #uuid "d315d1c5-713f-573c-8e7e-e10cf3b7508b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95c12fb3-5acd-5573-a477-4562c842d9b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-105681" :question/problem "![](images/5a/5a9564ba5a69f4bafa8ff85275ee009b133101aa36c682dd3e50efc949c76d92.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=-4+5y-{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis, as shown above. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4+5y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-5y+4 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=1,\\,4.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=1$ to $y=4.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}y(-4+5y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49361606-6120-5b9c-888f-0cdaf012f125" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b6a583d-c7ca-5341-8533-78d10b205d96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}y\\sqrt{-6+5y-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530918} {:answer/id #uuid "36142af1-4277-5631-afe9-37489dbc8dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}y\\sqrt{-6+5y-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530982} {:answer/id #uuid "61d31fb5-3fa5-56c4-b3eb-78b0cadb7b76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}\\sqrt{-6+5y-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531076} {:answer/id #uuid "758b93bf-e6ed-5af8-9b01-44c043df134d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}(-6+5y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531122} {:answer/id #uuid "eff7a488-ad07-5710-adc9-b07871f78778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}(-6+5y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36142af1-4277-5631-afe9-37489dbc8dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}y\\sqrt{-6+5y-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530982} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a153ace-3e22-541c-9fa2-ba67f8c82867" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba5c4320-47ed-5bb0-ac9c-df1a49799343" :question/math-academy-id "q-105733" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=\\sqrt{-6+5y-{y}^{2}}$ and the $y$-axis. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-6+5y-{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ -6+5y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ -({y}^{2}-5y+6) & =0 \\\\ -(y-2)(y-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=2$ and $y=3.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=2$ to $y=3.$\n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}y\\sqrt{-6+5y-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49361606-6120-5b9c-888f-0cdaf012f125" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57b88155-699b-5d53-b1ab-30536f6a3ca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}y(y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531058} {:answer/id #uuid "6ae364df-1e16-5dda-81a1-fd58f62da5fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531102} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9b062f-5840-5d1b-986d-1b39cbeafdbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(y-2{y}^{2})}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531139} {:answer/id #uuid "ac998975-6c85-534b-b1a2-c8c89b1565da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{(y-2{y}^{2})}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531236} {:answer/id #uuid "b69e2f70-b6fa-5b42-b438-05a6ef6f2697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57b88155-699b-5d53-b1ab-30536f6a3ca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}y(y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531058} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c1fb730-89df-5930-9ffd-4e3c7d99d7df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e4f431c-c20b-5b36-b61b-0c45e3b37d9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-11403" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=y-2{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(1-2y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}y(y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531648 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49361606-6120-5b9c-888f-0cdaf012f125" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44f4f718-3229-5c1c-a6c6-14499190e1b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{(2y-{