e curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(2-y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0,\\,2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49361606-6120-5b9c-888f-0cdaf012f125" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23e64d86-05a9-5382-8c0a-8a18e65807bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}y(-3+4y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530944} {:answer/id #uuid "2746730c-99ed-5dd7-b005-677ed8644940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{(-3+4y-{y}^{2})}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530954} {:answer/id #uuid "bcdc6463-6f32-5d66-8da7-95ff3a36dfb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}{(-3+4y-{y}^{2})}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531264} {:answer/id #uuid "ca20c5e1-7e01-5336-993d-49aa824f3191" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{y}^{2}(-3+4y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531286} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f7f47c-8665-51b7-83f7-b83cd4d703f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}y(-3+4y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23e64d86-05a9-5382-8c0a-8a18e65807bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}y(-3+4y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530944} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b615012-8d63-5680-878a-26a4ccb9be61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e9d38c5-007b-59fb-ae42-189521635fd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-126100" :question/problem "![](images/cc/cc7b2e67a5137b32917d8b96e3a53f66266e3ecabc2738b2a42417e87664457b.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=-3+4y-{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis, as shown above. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3+4y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y+3 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=1,\\,3.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=1$ to $y=3.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}y(-3+4y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "623f9081-96b8-53f5-8881-b17bea5d0316" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03592d71-01c8-5c8e-8dc1-555c9e33cf9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530867} {:answer/id #uuid "0c15f7c1-4bcc-5b00-9a47-f77a64d0f457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530886} {:answer/id #uuid "3b94c6a5-9527-5477-a1a3-7f03234197e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530990} {:answer/id #uuid "432c08ab-5b41-5def-a73b-e813b7482639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531005} {:answer/id #uuid "7732a064-ce3b-5465-88a9-cfdd49f7a517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b94c6a5-9527-5477-a1a3-7f03234197e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530990} :answer-field/id #uuid "1289d38d-0117-531f-a4f5-10acc347ae1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2fefe340-5c6b-584d-b7e8-9e9204c610ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-126111" :question/problem "![](images/28/287bde55681f44bd4132fdd5b4777a66d3dba7b16708533d755379c41f35b797.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=-3+4y-{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis, as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3+4y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y+3 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=1,$ and $y=3.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=1$ to $y=3.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}y(-3+4y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}(-3y+4{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (-3{\\int }_{1}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y+4{\\int }_{1}^{3}{y}^{2}\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{1}^{3}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi (-3\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{1}^{3}+4\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{1}^{3}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{1}^{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (-3(\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})+4(\\frac{27}{3}-\\frac{1}{3})-(\\frac{81}{4}-\\frac{1}{4})) \\\\  & =2\\pi (-12+\\frac{104}{3}-20) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "623f9081-96b8-53f5-8881-b17bea5d0316" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db6ee6f-edbb-5e5c-b2d1-764a2ea9a097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{150}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0db6ee6f-edbb-5e5c-b2d1-764a2ea9a097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{150}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530889} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7d44499-1aa4-52d0-a15f-ec7d8581275c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d1d4e63-93d8-5dc2-a21f-3d16bb6f6063" :question/math-academy-id "q-352098" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7d6ef1d9a58af96b51152ce354ec85c616921187ed467273e8c41c4fe93fc19c.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $x={y}^{2}-5{y}^{4}$ and the $y$-axis (shown in the picture). Using the shell method, calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-5{y}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(1-5{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ y & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}y({y}^{2}-5{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}({y}^{3}-5{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-5{\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}-\\frac{5}{6}{y}^{6}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}\\,) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{25}-0)-\\frac{5}{6}(\\frac{1}{125}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{100}-\\frac{1}{150}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{300}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{150}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "623f9081-96b8-53f5-8881-b17bea5d0316" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e920f28-4383-54cc-9cc9-d78d86338f95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{160}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e920f28-4383-54cc-9cc9-d78d86338f95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{160}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530967} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c795d78-7949-57ee-a8d5-8e5d26c90629" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e282fab-f673-50f6-9e1f-aaa485da50b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-352100" :question/problem "![](images/69/69dbf7a007980f1f1a9c2531ad398237e22d28f329afc619e05b8372afe970d4.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $x={y}^{2}-2{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis (shown in the picture). Using the shell method, calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(1-2y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,1-2y=0 \\\\ y & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}y({y}^{2}-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}({y}^{3}-2{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2}-\\frac{2}{5}{y}^{5}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2}\\,) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{16}-0)-\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{1}{32}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{64}-\\frac{1}{80}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{320}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{160}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "623f9081-96b8-53f5-8881-b17bea5d0316" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85964491-e360-5d94-bffc-8e0d1ef8230f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85964491-e360-5d94-bffc-8e0d1ef8230f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531156} :answer-field/id #uuid "0682446c-0c6a-5d39-8e65-0e1157e67525" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a00b0b94-53a1-5e23-84f6-859ac793fc56" :question/math-academy-id "q-362710" :question/problem "![](images/3d/3dbd6e325acdddddfb0bdcca5eef77fc3e75df2ccf946592780a0e1043ee1b01.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $x=-\\sqrt{y}+y$ and the $y$-axis from $y=0$ to $y=1$ (shown in the picture). Using the shell method, calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated about the $x$-axis.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The region is enclosed between the $y$-axis, the curve $x=-\\sqrt{y}+y$, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nSince the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis, the width of the cylindrical shells is given by $f(y)=0-(-\\sqrt{y}+y)=\\sqrt{y}-y.$ Using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(\\sqrt{y}-y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3/2}-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi ({\\frac{2{y}^{5/2}}{5}|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =2\\pi ((\\frac{2}{5}-0)-(\\frac{1}{3}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "623f9081-96b8-53f5-8881-b17bea5d0316" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b2bc8ac-9cce-5432-82e4-a0d93044820f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b2bc8ac-9cce-5432-82e4-a0d93044820f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530916} :answer-field/id #uuid "a692812a-555a-5732-b8c5-43f45d5cfb92" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69689ccc-dfd4-5812-991b-d48dbc7eafee" :question/math-academy-id "q-363229" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=5y-5{y}^{4}$ and the $y$-axis. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-5{y}^{4} & =0 \\\\ 5y(1-{y}^{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(5y-5{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(5{y}^{2}-5{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{5}{3}{y}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-\\frac{5}{6}{y}^{6}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\,) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{5}{3}(1-0)-\\frac{5}{6}(1-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{5}{3}-\\frac{5}{6}) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{5}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
